   "text": "This drawing is the sodium salt of deoxycholic acid (sodium deoxycholate), a bile-acid steroid. I built the SMILES from the cholic acid skeleton and removed the 7-OH, then checked the stereocentres I could against the drawing.\n\nFeatures read from the drawing:\n- **Skeleton:** a four-ring steroid core with two angular methyl groups, drawn as wedges at C10 and C13.\n- **Side chain:** a branched chain at C17 ending in a carboxylate (C(=O)[O-]), with a wedged methyl at C20.\n- **Hydroxyl groups:** two OH groups, both hashed (\u03b1), at C3 and C12. There is no OH at C7, which distinguishes it from cholic acid.\n- **Ring-junction hydrogens:** wedged H at C5 (5\u03b2, cis A/B ring fusion) and at C8, hashed H at C9 and C14.\n- **Counter-ion:** Na\u207a.\n\nI worked out the chirality tags at C3 and C5 directly from the drawing, and both matched the cholic acid template. I did not independently re-derive the remaining centres from the image.\n\nSMILES: C[C@H](CCC(=O)[O-])[C@H]1CC[C@@H]2[C@@]1([C@H](C[C@H]3[C@H]2CC[C@H]4[C@@]3(CC[C@H](C4)O)C)O)C.[Na+]"
